the dynamical rule, given by Equation 28, for choosing the step size $\eta_{t}$. We shall argue that this dynamical rule can be understood to approximate a heuristic algorithm for achieving, but not exceeding, what we shall call escape velocity:

• Choose a very small initial step size $\eta_{0}$—small enough to be a priori sure that $\eta_{0}\ll\eta_{\star}$, where $\eta_{\star}$ denotes escape velocity: the unknown but optimal initial step size;

• At each step, check if the weights ${\bm{w}}_{t}$ have escaped the linearization of the loss around the initial weights ${\bm{w}}_{0}$—if not, double the step size according to $\eta_{t+1}=2\times\eta_{t}$;

• Once the weights ${\bm{w}}_{t}$ have escaped the initial linearization, stop increasing the step size. We say that the step size $\eta_{t}$ has reached escape velocity $\eta_{\star}$.
